<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Quadi</h1><p class="st-meta">10 BCE – 406 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Suebian people north of the middle Danube who, with the Marcomanni, fought Rome under Marcus Aurelius and later joined the great migration into Spain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Quadi settled in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-moravia">Moravia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, probably moving there from the Main region around the same time as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marcomanni">Marcomanni</a> at the end of the 1st century BCE. They formed part of the realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> and later of the client kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannius">Vannius</a>, established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 19.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ruled by kings, often confirmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the Quadi lived in villages, traded with Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and buried their elite with Roman imports. They joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> from 166; the 'Rain Miracle' depicted on the Column of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> took place on their territory. Rome built forward posts in their land such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musov">Mušov</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 374 the killing of their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabinius-king-of-the-quadi">Gabinius</a> by a Roman officer provoked a raid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> died in 375 while confronting their envoys. In 406 many Quadi crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alans">Alans</a>, probably becoming part of the Suebian kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/quadi.jpg" alt="Quadi" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: Zello · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quadi_settlement_area.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>10 BCE – 406 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gabinius, Ariogaesus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elbe Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=198&amp;focus=quadi" aria-label="Quadi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5122 468 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Quadi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5442 724h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5442 724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Slovakia. <a href="/atlas?year=198&amp;focus=quadi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
